# Line endings, decided here rather than by whatever each machine defaults to.
#
# .editorconfig already says end_of_line = lf, but that only instructs editors — it has no say
# in what git writes on checkout. On Windows, core.autocrlf defaults to true, so every file
# arrived with CRLF and a test that matched on "$" silently found nothing, on a machine where
# CI could never reproduce it. This is the layer that actually enforces the rule.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts are run by bash and must never carry CR, which becomes part of the last argument
# on a line and produces errors that name a command with an invisible character in it.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Files git should never translate. text=auto detects most of these on its own; naming them
# means a corrupted image can never be blamed on line-ending conversion.
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
